1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,640
Look out of any window at night,
and you'll see the result

2
00:00:03,650 --> 00:00:06,210
of a battle fought more than a
hundred years ago.

3
00:00:06,917 --> 00:00:09,287
It was a fight over how
electricity should travel,

4
00:00:09,297 --> 00:00:11,397
from the power station to your
home.

5
00:00:12,167 --> 00:00:15,167
On one side was the most famous
inventor in the world,

6
00:00:15,587 --> 00:00:16,387
Thomas Edison.

7
00:00:17,127 --> 00:00:20,537
On the other, a brilliant
immigrant engineer named Nikola

8
00:00:20,547 --> 00:00:21,077
Tesla,

9
00:00:21,087 --> 00:00:23,747
and the businessman George
Westinghouse.

10
00:00:24,583 --> 00:00:27,893
The winner shaped the electrical
grid that powers almost

11
00:00:27,903 --> 00:00:29,363
the entire world today.

12
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:34,230
In eighteen seventy nine,
Edison's lab in Menlo

13
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:35,040
Park,

14
00:00:35,160 --> 00:00:38,610
New Jersey, produced a practical
electric light bulb that could

15
00:00:38,620 --> 00:00:39,520
glow for hours.

16
00:00:40,417 --> 00:00:43,637
His team tested thousands of
materials for the glowing
filament,

17
00:00:44,117 --> 00:00:47,547
from cotton thread to fishing
line, before settling on

18
00:00:47,557 --> 00:00:50,827
carbonised bamboo, which could
last for well over a

19
00:00:50,837 --> 00:00:51,657
thousand hours.

20
00:00:52,417 --> 00:00:55,587
Edison would eventually hold
more than a thousand American

21
00:00:55,597 --> 00:00:56,397
patents.

22
00:00:56,777 --> 00:00:59,877
Newspapers called him the Wizard
of Menlo Park.

23
00:01:00,667 --> 00:01:03,987
But a light bulb is useless
without electricity to run it.

24
00:01:04,347 --> 00:01:07,247
So Edison designed a whole
system: generators,

25
00:01:07,927 --> 00:01:10,687
cables, switches, fuses and
meters.

26
00:01:11,583 --> 00:01:14,993
On the fourth of September,
eighteen eighty two,

27
00:01:15,003 --> 00:01:18,053
his Pearl Street power station
in Manhattan switched

28
00:01:18,063 --> 00:01:18,863
on,

29
00:01:18,983 --> 00:01:21,703
lighting buildings across a few
city blocks.

30
00:01:22,667 --> 00:01:25,937
Its huge steam-driven generators
were nicknamed Jumbos,

31
00:01:25,947 --> 00:01:28,347
after a famous circus elephant.

32
00:01:28,987 --> 00:01:31,227
Each one weighed about twenty
seven tons.

33
00:01:32,167 --> 00:01:34,717
At first, it supplied about four
hundred lamps,

34
00:01:34,727 --> 00:01:36,527
for around eighty customers.

35
00:01:37,487 --> 00:01:40,607
Within two years, it was
lighting more than ten thousand.

36
00:01:41,250 --> 00:01:44,370
Edison's system used direct
current, or DC.

37
00:01:45,050 --> 00:01:47,850
The electricity flows steadily
in one direction,

38
00:01:48,330 --> 00:01:49,490
like water through a pipe.

39
00:01:50,250 --> 00:01:52,350
But DC had a serious problem.

40
00:01:52,990 --> 00:01:54,170
Sent through long wires,

41
00:01:54,810 --> 00:01:56,910
much of the power was lost as
heat.

42
00:01:57,667 --> 00:02:01,097
At the low voltages Edison used,
a power station

43
00:02:01,107 --> 00:02:03,927
could only serve customers
within about a mile.

44
00:02:04,367 --> 00:02:07,767
A city would need a power
station in nearly every
neighbourhood.

45
00:02:09,483 --> 00:02:12,673
Nikola Tesla was born in
eighteen fifty six,

46
00:02:12,683 --> 00:02:16,083
in what is now Croatia, to a
Serbian family.

47
00:02:16,917 --> 00:02:20,367
He had a remarkable memory, and
said he could design and

48
00:02:20,377 --> 00:02:22,757
test machines entirely in his
imagination.

49
00:02:23,833 --> 00:02:26,593
As a student, he became obsessed
with an idea:

50
00:02:27,133 --> 00:02:29,443
a motor that ran on alternating
current,

51
00:02:29,453 --> 00:02:32,703
AC, where the electricity
rapidly switches

52
00:02:32,713 --> 00:02:34,333
direction back and forth.

53
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:38,410
In eighteen eighty four, he
arrived in New York with almost

54
00:02:38,420 --> 00:02:38,950
nothing,

55
00:02:38,960 --> 00:02:40,480
and went to work for Edison.

56
00:02:41,333 --> 00:02:42,233
According to Tesla,

57
00:02:43,073 --> 00:02:46,123
Edison promised him fifty
thousand dollars if he could
improve

58
00:02:46,133 --> 00:02:47,633
the company's dynamos.

59
00:02:48,033 --> 00:02:51,243
When he did, Edison told him:
you don't understand our

60
00:02:51,253 --> 00:02:52,173
American humour.

61
00:02:52,917 --> 00:02:54,457
But the two men were opposites.

62
00:02:55,097 --> 00:02:58,507
Edison was a tireless tinkerer,
who tested thousands of ideas

63
00:02:58,517 --> 00:03:00,137
by trial and error.

64
00:03:00,657 --> 00:03:01,937
Tesla was a theorist.

65
00:03:02,757 --> 00:03:04,917
They soon fell out, and Tesla
quit.

66
00:03:05,667 --> 00:03:08,347
For a while, he even dug ditches
to survive.

67
00:03:09,250 --> 00:03:12,600
Then, in eighteen eighty eight,
he patented his AC induction

68
00:03:12,610 --> 00:03:13,410
motor.

69
00:03:13,917 --> 00:03:17,267
That May, he demonstrated it in
a famous lecture to the

70
00:03:17,277 --> 00:03:19,857
American Institute of Electrical
Engineers.

71
00:03:20,833 --> 00:03:23,553
Instead of brushes and sparking
contacts,

72
00:03:24,073 --> 00:03:27,343
it used coils that create a
magnetic field which spins

73
00:03:27,353 --> 00:03:28,153
around.

74
00:03:28,413 --> 00:03:30,753
The rotor is simply dragged
around by it.

75
00:03:31,417 --> 00:03:33,457
It was simple, tough and
efficient.

76
00:03:34,077 --> 00:03:36,957
Versions of it still run most of
the world's fans,

77
00:03:37,497 --> 00:03:40,447
pumps, factory machines and many
electric

78
00:03:40,457 --> 00:03:41,257
cars.

79
00:03:42,817 --> 00:03:45,717
The real advantage of AC was
something else:

80
00:03:46,077 --> 00:03:46,877
the transformer.

81
00:03:47,667 --> 00:03:50,817
A transformer is two coils of
wire wrapped

82
00:03:50,827 --> 00:03:52,247
around an iron core.

83
00:03:52,807 --> 00:03:54,407
Thanks to Faraday's induction,

84
00:03:55,047 --> 00:03:58,477
the changing current in one coil
creates current in the

85
00:03:58,487 --> 00:03:59,287
other.

86
00:03:59,417 --> 00:04:02,737
Give the second coil more turns,
and the voltage goes up.

87
00:04:03,197 --> 00:04:05,437
Give it fewer, and the voltage
goes down.

88
00:04:05,997 --> 00:04:08,037
It only works with alternating
current.

89
00:04:08,833 --> 00:04:09,853
Here's why that matters.

90
00:04:10,573 --> 00:04:13,813
The power lost as heat in a wire
depends on the current.

91
00:04:14,453 --> 00:04:17,643
Raise the voltage a hundred
times, and the current falls a

92
00:04:17,653 --> 00:04:19,813
hundred times, for the same
power.

93
00:04:20,500 --> 00:04:23,360
And because heat losses depend
on the current squared,

94
00:04:23,860 --> 00:04:26,140
the losses drop ten thousand
times.

95
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:29,900
So with AC, you can step the
voltage up,

96
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:33,160
send power hundreds of
kilometres along thin wires,

97
00:04:33,620 --> 00:04:35,380
and step it down again near
homes.

98
00:04:36,250 --> 00:04:39,410
In the mid eighteen eighties,
engineers in Hungary,

99
00:04:40,070 --> 00:04:42,650
Britain and the United States
developed practical

100
00:04:43,250 --> 00:04:44,050
transformers.

101
00:04:44,410 --> 00:04:47,840
In eighteen eighty six, William
Stanley lit up the main

102
00:04:47,850 --> 00:04:50,790
street of Great Barrington,
Massachusetts,

103
00:04:51,450 --> 00:04:52,570
with alternating current.

104
00:04:54,317 --> 00:04:57,667
George Westinghouse, an inventor
and industrialist who

105
00:04:57,677 --> 00:05:00,157
had made a fortune from railway
air brakes,

106
00:05:00,477 --> 00:05:01,337
saw the future.

107
00:05:01,877 --> 00:05:04,497
He bought Tesla's patents, and
hired him.

108
00:05:05,167 --> 00:05:08,457
Tesla reportedly received about
sixty thousand dollars

109
00:05:08,467 --> 00:05:11,897
in cash and stock, plus a
royalty for

110
00:05:11,907 --> 00:05:14,987
every horsepower of AC equipment
sold.

111
00:05:15,750 --> 00:05:18,110
Edison had invested everything
in DC.

112
00:05:18,830 --> 00:05:22,240
He began a campaign to convince
the public that AC's high

113
00:05:22,250 --> 00:05:23,450
voltages were deadly.

114
00:05:24,250 --> 00:05:27,130
His allies staged gruesome
public demonstrations,

115
00:05:27,650 --> 00:05:30,650
killing animals with AC to
frighten people.

116
00:05:31,417 --> 00:05:34,367
And when New York State
introduced the electric chair
for

117
00:05:34,377 --> 00:05:35,177
executions,

118
00:05:35,757 --> 00:05:38,567
it used an AC generator, partly
to tie

119
00:05:38,577 --> 00:05:40,477
Westinghouse's name to death.

120
00:05:41,083 --> 00:05:44,533
The first execution, of a man
named William Kemmler

121
00:05:44,543 --> 00:05:47,243
in eighteen ninety, was horribly
botched.

122
00:05:47,883 --> 00:05:50,893
Westinghouse remarked that they
would have done better using an

123
00:05:50,903 --> 00:05:51,703
axe.

124
00:05:52,083 --> 00:05:54,623
But it was true that high
voltage needed respect.

125
00:05:55,223 --> 00:05:57,523
Overhead wires in cities were a
tangled,

126
00:05:57,903 --> 00:06:01,123
dangerous mess, and people were
killed by fallen lines.

127
00:06:02,083 --> 00:06:04,483
Meanwhile, AC kept proving
itself.

128
00:06:05,103 --> 00:06:08,143
In eighteen ninety one, a mine
in Telluride,

129
00:06:08,443 --> 00:06:09,243
Colorado,

130
00:06:09,423 --> 00:06:12,533
began running on AC power from a
hydroelectric

131
00:06:12,543 --> 00:06:14,563
plant several kilometres away.

132
00:06:15,250 --> 00:06:18,520
That same year in Germany,
engineers sent three-phase

133
00:06:18,530 --> 00:06:20,760
AC a hundred and seventy five
kilometres,

134
00:06:20,770 --> 00:06:22,410
from Lauffen to Frankfurt.

135
00:06:24,400 --> 00:06:27,590
The turning point came in
eighteen ninety three.

136
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:30,790
Westinghouse won the contract to
light the World's

137
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:33,020
Columbian Exposition in Chicago,

138
00:06:33,620 --> 00:06:35,900
underbidding its rival, General
Electric.

139
00:06:36,750 --> 00:06:40,160
Visitors saw a glittering White
City, lit by tens

140
00:06:40,170 --> 00:06:43,200
of thousands of electric lamps,
all running on

141
00:06:43,210 --> 00:06:44,310
alternating current.

142
00:06:45,010 --> 00:06:47,350
Tesla's machines were among the
stars of the show.

143
00:06:48,083 --> 00:06:51,523
That same year, Westinghouse won
the biggest prize of all:

144
00:06:51,923 --> 00:06:54,323
a contract to harness Niagara
Falls.

145
00:06:55,167 --> 00:06:58,077
The falling water spun huge
turbines, and

146
00:06:58,087 --> 00:07:01,297
Tesla-designed AC generators
turned that motion

147
00:07:01,307 --> 00:07:02,207
into electricity.

148
00:07:03,333 --> 00:07:06,183
The first three generators each
produced five thousand

149
00:07:06,193 --> 00:07:06,993
horsepower,

150
00:07:07,333 --> 00:07:09,753
the most powerful in the world
at the time.

151
00:07:10,500 --> 00:07:13,323
On the sixteenth of November,
eighteen ninety

152
00:07:13,333 --> 00:07:14,070
six,

153
00:07:14,080 --> 00:07:17,020
power from Niagara was switched
on in Buffalo,

154
00:07:17,620 --> 00:07:19,360
about forty kilometres away.

155
00:07:20,167 --> 00:07:23,297
Streetcars ran and factories
hummed on

156
00:07:23,307 --> 00:07:26,787
electricity generated by a
waterfall, miles away.

157
00:07:27,267 --> 00:07:29,647
The war of the currents was
effectively over.

158
00:07:30,087 --> 00:07:31,047
AC had won.

159
00:07:32,650 --> 00:07:36,070
Edison's company merged with
others to form General Electric,

160
00:07:36,670 --> 00:07:38,710
which soon embraced AC too.

161
00:07:39,250 --> 00:07:41,670
Westinghouse's company struggled
with debts.

162
00:07:42,670 --> 00:07:46,020
Tesla famously tore up a
contract that would have paid
him

163
00:07:46,030 --> 00:07:49,380
royalties on every AC motor
sold, to help

164
00:07:49,390 --> 00:07:50,610
keep the firm afloat.

165
00:07:51,430 --> 00:07:53,030
That story is often told,

166
00:07:53,690 --> 00:07:55,430
though historians debate the
details.

167
00:07:56,417 --> 00:07:59,057
Tesla went on to experiment with
radio,

168
00:07:59,557 --> 00:08:02,627
wireless power and giant coils
that threw

169
00:08:02,637 --> 00:08:04,317
lightning across his lab.

170
00:08:04,917 --> 00:08:08,307
But he died poor and alone in a
New York hotel room

171
00:08:08,317 --> 00:08:10,037
in nineteen forty three.

172
00:08:10,500 --> 00:08:13,500
Today, the unit of magnetic
field strength,

173
00:08:13,900 --> 00:08:15,680
the tesla, carries his name.

174
00:08:17,317 --> 00:08:20,547
Today, almost every power grid
on Earth runs

175
00:08:20,557 --> 00:08:23,987
on alternating current,
switching direction fifty or

176
00:08:23,997 --> 00:08:25,697
sixty times every second.

177
00:08:26,417 --> 00:08:29,747
Westinghouse's engineers picked
sixty cycles a

178
00:08:29,757 --> 00:08:30,917
second for America.

179
00:08:31,477 --> 00:08:33,577
Much of the rest of the world
settled on fifty.

180
00:08:34,117 --> 00:08:36,437
That's why some appliances don't
work when you travel.

181
00:08:37,333 --> 00:08:40,643
Giant transformers step the
voltage up to hundreds of
thousands

182
00:08:40,653 --> 00:08:43,733
of volts for long journeys
across the country,

183
00:08:44,073 --> 00:08:46,553
then step it down to the voltage
in your sockets.

184
00:08:47,583 --> 00:08:51,013
In a twist Edison might have
enjoyed, very long distance

185
00:08:51,023 --> 00:08:53,633
links now often use direct
current again,

186
00:08:53,643 --> 00:08:56,953
at extremely high voltages,
thanks to modern

187
00:08:56,963 --> 00:08:57,763
electronics.

188
00:08:58,583 --> 00:09:02,083
But the basic system,
generators, transformers

189
00:09:02,263 --> 00:09:05,573
and alternating current, is the
one Tesla and

190
00:09:05,583 --> 00:09:07,003
Westinghouse fought for.

191
00:09:07,750 --> 00:09:11,200
And even now, hundreds of
millions of people still live

192
00:09:11,210 --> 00:09:12,210
without electricity.

193
00:09:12,970 --> 00:09:16,190
The job that began in eighteen
eighty two isn't finished.

194
00:09:17,167 --> 00:09:19,567
And when the grid fails,
everyone notices.

195
00:09:20,387 --> 00:09:23,877
In August two thousand and
three, a software bug and

196
00:09:23,887 --> 00:09:27,097
overgrown trees in Ohio set off
a blackout

197
00:09:27,107 --> 00:09:30,417
that left about fifty five
million people in the United

198
00:09:30,427 --> 00:09:32,447
States and Canada in the dark.

199
00:09:34,067 --> 00:09:36,127
Edison gave the world the light
bulb.

200
00:09:36,587 --> 00:09:39,777
But it was alternating current
that carried the light into
every

201
00:09:39,787 --> 00:09:40,587
home.
